{"id":"23897d66-20cb-4552-825e-c4fd6b81416d","object":"chat.completion","created":1785012896,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"from collections import Counter, defaultdict\\n\\ndef solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = defaultdict(list)\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        p = n.get('parent')\\n        if p is not None:\\n            children[p].append(n['id'])\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    symbol_counts = Counter(n['symbol'] for n in nodes)\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removed = []\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in children.get(nid, [])):\\n            continue\\n        sym = n['symbol']\\n        if sym in required and symbol_counts[sym] == 1:\\n            continue\\n        removed.append(nid)\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removed, 'retained_node_ids': retained_sorted}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to parse the task carefully. This is \"SE-PE-01\" which seems to be about grammar-guided node reduction of a parse tree. The payload contains nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols. The goal is to remove nodes under certain constraints. Need to produce removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nFrom the description: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So process by depth descending. \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we check that after removal, the set of symbols in the retained tree still includes all required_symbols. Also \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So root (depth 0) is not removable. Also nodes with required symbol? Actually \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" means if a node's symbol is in required_symbols, it cannot be removed? Or does it mean nodes that are required based on some condition? The phrase \"required symbol\" suggests the symbol value is required. So nodes whose symbol is in required_symbols cannot be removed. Also \"node with retained children\" means if a node has children that are still retained, you cannot remove it? Or you can only remove leaf nodes? The description: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if a node has any children that are still in the tree (i.e., not removed), you cannot remove it. So we can only remove removable leaf nodes that are not required symbol and not root.\n\nAlso \"removable\" field indicates if it's allowed to be removed. But \"Never remove\" constraints might override? Possibly removable is a prerequisite. The description says \"Tentatively remove a node only if ...\" So removable must be true, and also constraints.\n\nImplementation approach: Build tree structure from nodes list. Identify root (parent null). Build mapping id->node. Also children mapping. Then traverse depth descending. For each node, if removable is True and node symbol not in required_symbols and node has no children that are still in the tree (i.e., all children have been removed or never existed), then attempt to remove it: check that after removal, all required symbols are still present in the remaining tree. If yes, remove it (add to removed list). Then update tree (remove from parent's children). Continue.\n\nOrder: deeper first (descending depth), and stable tie break by node ID (string). So sort candidates by depth descending, then by id ascending? \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" Probably ascending order.\n\nWe need to produce removed_node_ids (list of IDs in order of removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted list of IDs of nodes that remain, possibly sorted? \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids should be sorted (presumably lexicographically or by id? \"sorted\" likely by ID ascending). The removal order is as we removed them.\n\nWe must ensure we don't mutate case, but we can create copies.\n\nNow, implement solve(case) where case is a dict with \"nodes\" and \"required_symbols\". Return dict with removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nWe need to handle the \"required_symbols\" check: After tentative removal, check that every required symbol is still present in some retained node. That means we need to update the set of symbols in the tree after removals.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Build node dict from nodes list: node = {id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility? Not needed?}. Also children list.\n2. Identify root: node with parent None.\n3. Initialize retained set: all node ids.\n4. Build initial symbol set: set of symbol for each node.\n5. Process nodes in order: sort nodes by (-depth, id). For each node:\n   if node.id not in retained: continue (already removed)\n   if node.removable is False: continue\n   if node.symbol in required_symbols: continue\n   # Check if node has any children that are still retained: if any child id in retained: continue\n   # Tentatively remove: compute new symbol set = current symbols minus node.symbol if it's the only node with that symbol? Actually we need to ensure all required symbols remain. So we need to check if removing this node would remove a required symbol. Since we already skip if node.symbol in required_symbols, that's not enough because other nodes might have that required symbol. But the condition states \"only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we need to ensure that after removal, there is still at least one node for each required symbol. Since node.symbol might be required but there may be other nodes with that symbol. So we need to check: for each required symbol, is there at least one retained node with that symbol other than this node? Or if this node is the only one, then cannot remove. So we must check: if node.symbol in required_symbols, then after removal, the count of nodes with that symbol must be >= 1. That means we need to know if there are other nodes with same symbol still retained. So we need a count per symbol. So maintain a symbol count dict. Initially count per symbol from all nodes. When removing a node, decrement its symbol count. If that count would become 0 and symbol is required, then can't remove. But we already skip if node.symbol in required_symbols, but there might be other nodes with same symbol, so it's fine to remove if other nodes remain. So we need to handle the check more precisely.\n\nBetter: Maintain a symbol set (or multiset) of retained nodes. For required symbols, we need that after removal, the symbol still exists. So we can check: if node.symbol in required_symbols and count[node.symbol] == 1, then cannot remove (since this is the only node). Else we can.\n\nAlso need to check children: if node has children in retained, cannot remove.\n\nAlso ensure node is removable field is True.\n\nAdditionally, \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So we enforce that.\n\nAlgorithm:\n\n- Build list of nodes, map id to node.\n- Build children lists: for each node, children list.\n- Initialize retained_ids = set(all node ids)\n- Initialize symbol_counts = Counter(symbol of each node)\n- Sort all node ids by (-depth, id). Since we need to process each node potentially multiple times? Actually we iterate once; after removal, later nodes' children condition may change. That's fine because we process by depth descending; once a parent is processed, its children are deeper. After removing a leaf, its parent may become leaf later. But since we process deeper first, when we reach parent, its children may have been removed. So we need to check children condition based on current retained set. So we can compute children condition on the fly.\n\nImplementation: nodes_sorted = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id'])). Then for each node in that list:\n   if node['id'] not in retained_ids: continue\n   if not node['removable']: continue\n   # check children: if any child id in retained_ids: continue\n   # check required symbols: if node['symbol'] in required_symbols and symbol_counts[node['symbol']] == 1: continue\n   # else, we can remove: \n   removed_node_ids.append(node['id'])\n   retained_ids.remove(node['id'])\n   symbol_counts[node['symbol']] -= 1\n   # also, we might need to update parent's children? Not needed because we check child presence via retained_ids. The parent's children list is not modified; we just check against retained_ids.\n\nAfter loop, retained_node_ids = sorted(list(retained_ids)).\nReturn {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_node_ids, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids}.\n\nCheck examples? We don't have expected outputs, but we can test with provided fixture. First case: nodes with depths, etc. Let's manually compute for first case to see if algorithm makes sense.\n\nCase 0 payload:\nnodes: \nroot depth0 removable false symbol root\nn1 depth1 removable false symbol s1\nn2 depth1 removable true symbol s2\nn3 depth1 removable false symbol s0\nn4 depth2 removable true symbol s1 (parent n2)\nn5 depth2 removable false symbol s2 (parent n3)\nn6 depth2 removable true symbol s0 (parent n1)\nrequired_symbols: root, s1\n\nProcess depth descending: depth2 nodes: n4, n5, n6 (order by id: n4, n5, n6)\n- n4: removable true, symbol s1 (required). Count s1: nodes with s1: n1 and n4? n1 has symbol s1, n4 has s1. So count=2. If we remove n4, count becomes 1 -> still preserved. Check children? n4 depth2, any children? No children listed. So can remove? But n4's children? None. So yes, remove n4.\n- n5: removable false, skip.\n- n6: removable true, symbol s0 (not required). Check children: none. Count s0: n3 and n6? n3 s0, n6 s0. Remove n6, count s0 becomes 1, not required. So remove n6.\n\nNow depth1 nodes: n1, n2, n3 (order n1, n2, n3 by id)\n- n1: removable false, skip.\n- n2: removable true, symbol s2 (not required). Children: n4 is child? Parent n2. n4 was removed, but we need to check if any child retained. n4 removed, so no children retained. So can remove? But check if any other children? None. So n2 can be removed. But also check required symbols: s2 isn't required. So remove n2.\n- n3: removable false, skip.\n\ndepth0 root: removable false, skip.\n\nRemoved: n4, n6, n2. Retained: root, n1, n3, n5. Sorted retained: n1, n3, n5, root? Actually sorted by id: n1, n3, n5, root. But root id 'root' probably alphabetically after? 'root' vs 'n1'? 'n1' < 'root'? In lexicographic, 'n' < 'r', so n1, n3, n5, root. That seems plausible.\n\nNow check if any required symbol missing? s1 is required. After removal, nodes with s1: n1 remains. root remains. So ok.\n\nSo algorithm works for this case. Good.\n\nEdge: what if a node has children but all children have been removed? Then it can be removed. That's fine.\n\nNow we need to produce Python code for solve(case). Must not use external libraries, only standard lib. Use collections.Counter for symbol counts.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n- case is a dict with keys 'nodes' (list of dicts) and 'required_symbols' (list).\n- Build a dict mapping id to node.\n- Build children mapping: for each node, add its id to parent's children list. But we only need to check children existence; easier: for each node, we can compute its children by scanning nodes where parent == node['id']. But we can precompute a dict child_ids = defaultdict(list). Then iterate over nodes to fill.\n- Then sort nodes by (-depth, id).\n- Maintain retained set, symbol Counter.\n- Process in that order.\n\nPotential issue: the node list may not be sorted; but we'll sort.\n\nReturn dict with removed_node_ids list and retained_node_ids sorted.\n\nNow, we need to output a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value as a string of Python source code. Ensure it's properly JSON-escaped.\n\nWe must not include any extraneous text. The entire response should be exactly the JSON.\n\nLet's craft the Python code. Use def solve(case): ... return result.\n\nWe'll import from collections import Counter, defaultdict. That's allowed.\n\nCode:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children = defaultdict(list)\n    for n in nodes:\n        p = n.get('parent')\n        if p is not None:\n            children[p].append(n['id'])\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\n    symbol_counts = Counter(n['symbol'] for n in nodes)\n    # sort nodes by depth descending, then id\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    removed = []\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        if not n['removable']:\n            continue\n        # check children\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in children.get(nid, [])):\n            continue\n        # check required symbol representation\n        sym = n['symbol']\n        if sym in required and symbol_counts[sym] == 1:\n            continue\n        # remove\n        removed.append(nid)\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted}\n\nThat should work.\n\nNow we must output the JSON. The response should be one JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value that string. We'll construct the JSON manually or using json.dumps in the response? But we are writing a text response, so we need to produce the JSON string itself. We'll write it carefully, escape newlines and quotes.\n\nI'll write the Python code as a single string with newlines escaped as \\n, backslashes as \\\\, quotes as \\\". I'll produce the JSON.\n\nLet's write:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"from collections import Counter, defaultdict\\n\\ndef solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = defaultdict(list)\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        p = n.get('parent')\\n        if p is not None:\\n            children[p].append(n['id'])\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    symbol_counts = Counter(n['symbol'] for n in nodes)\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removed = []\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in children.get(nid, [])):\\n            continue\\n        sym = n['symbol']\\n        if sym in required and symbol_counts[sym] == 1:\\n            continue\\n        removed.append(nid)\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed, \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_sorted}\"}\n\nMake sure to escape properly. Double check: inside the string, we have single quotes, which are fine. Backslashes: we have none. Newlines: we have \\n for each newline. The string has actual newlines? No, in JSON string we represent newline as \\n. So the above is fine.\n\nBut note: In the Python code, we have line breaks. We'll replace actual newlines with \\n and escape double quotes inside the string. But the code uses single quotes, so we don't need to escape double quotes. However, the outer JSON uses double quotes for keys and string values. So the inner string (Python code) is enclosed in double quotes. Within that, we have double quotes only for the f-string? No, the Python code uses double quotes in the return statement? Actually it uses double quotes for the dictionary keys: {\"removed_node_ids\": removed, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted}. Those inner double quotes must be escaped as \\\". Let's check: In the code I wrote: return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted}. That contains double quotes. So we need to escape them as \\\". Also in the assignment within the string? We have case['nodes'] uses single quotes, okay. So we need to escape the double quotes in the return statement.\n\nAlso in the 'required' assignment: required = set(case['required_symbols']) - single.\n\nThus the inner string must have \\\" for each double quote used in the Python code. Also backslashes: none.\n\nLet me rewrite the code to avoid double quotes by using single quotes for the return dict keys? That would be easier. Python allows single quotes for dict keys too. So we can write return {'removed_node_ids': removed, 'retained_node_ids': retained_sorted}. That avoids escaping double quotes. Good.\n\nNow the code uses only single quotes. So the inner string will have no double quotes except the outer boundary. Only need to escape newlines and backslashes. No backslashes. So we can just put literal newlines as \\n. That's fine.\n\nBut wait: The code also has dict literal in case['nodes'] etc. Single quotes. So no issue.\n\nThus final JSON string: {\"implementation\": \"from collections import Counter, defaultdict\\n\\ndef solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = defaultdict(list)\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        p = n.get('parent')\\n        if p is not None:\\n            children[p].append(n['id'])\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    symbol_counts = Counter(n['symbol'] for n in nodes)\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removed = []\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in children.get(nid, [])):\\n            continue\\n        sym = n['symbol']\\n        if sym in required and symbol_counts[sym] == 1:\\n            continue\\n        removed.append(nid)\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removed, 'retained_node_ids': retained_sorted}\"}\n\nNow we need to ensure this is valid JSON. We'll test in mind: The string contains no unescaped control characters. Newlines represented as \\n. Good.\n\nOne more check: The original task says \"Do not access files, the network, environment variables, subprocesses, randomness, or clocks.\" Our code does not.\n\nAlso \"must not mutate `case`\". We don't modify case.\n\nAll good.\n\nNow produce the final answer as a JSON object."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2085,"completion_tokens":4455,"total_tokens":6540,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4150},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":37},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}